course: dictionary
lesson: 9685
title: Suffering and what it means
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Suffering» can mean pain, sadness or difficulty that a person or animal feels. It can also come from the verb «suffer», as in «suffering from» an illness. Think of it as feeling bad in a big way.
explain: «She is suffering from a cold.» The words suffering from tell you what is wrong: she has a cold and feels unwell. Other phrases to notice are «end the suffering» and «pain and suffering», which both point to something painful or hard.
explain: Read the words around it to find the meaning. «Owning up quickly stops an innocent person suffering any longer.» The words innocent and stops tell you the person is not to blame and is in trouble, so suffering means being in pain or distress. Swap in "feeling pain" and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: «Suffering» means pain, sadness or difficulty that a person or animal feels.
fact: «Suffering from» means having an illness or a problem, like «suffering from flu».
fact: «End the suffering» means to stop someone or something being in pain.
fact: Suffering is also a noun, so you can have «suffering» or «sufferings»: things people go through.
fact: When a very ill animal is in pain, a vet can help it die peacefully, without pain. Families choose this to end its suffering.
exercise: «She is suffering from a cold.» What does suffering from mean here? | *Having an illness or problem | Winning a race | Feeling very happy and full of energy | Playing outside in the snow
exercise: «Buddhism aims to end suffering through wisdom and kindness.» What does end suffering mean? | Forget their homework | *Stop people being in pain or difficulty | Start a long journey | Make people feel bored
exercise: «When a pet is very old or ill and suffering, a vet can help it die peacefully.» Find and copy the word that tells you the pet is in pain. | pet | vet | *suffering | peacefully
test: «Owning up quickly stops an innocent person suffering any longer.» What is the best swap for suffering here? | Singing loudly | Sleeping soundly | Enjoying a game | *Being in pain
test: What does the phrase «pain and suffering» mean? | *Pain and difficulty that someone feels | A long walk in the park | Happiness and fun | Money and food
exam: Which sentence uses suffering to mean pain or difficulty? | The dog ran across the park | *Pain and suffering can be very hard to bear | We ate pizza for lunch | The sun was shining and the birds were singing
